(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["edema"]="<h1>Formation of Oedema</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-exactly-is-oedema\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> What exactly is oedema?</h2>\n<p><strong>Oedema</strong> is too much fluid in the spaces <strong>between the cells</strong> (the interstitium), enough to make tissue swell.</p>\n<p>Fluid is always leaving the capillaries, and the lymphatics are always carrying it back. <strong>Oedema happens when more leaves than can be carried away.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"edema-balance\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Fluid balance causes oedema\"><span><strong>Filtration ↑</strong><small>More fluid entering tissue</small></span><b>+</b><span><strong>Drainage insufficient</strong><small>Less fluid leaving tissue</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>Oedema</strong><small>Fluid accumulates</small></span></div>\n\n<p>So there are only two ways to get oedema: <strong>too much filtration</strong>, or <strong>too little drainage</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-what-physical-forces-govern-filtration\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> What physical forces govern filtration?</h2>\n<p>The four Starling forces decide how much fluid leaves the capillaries <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation?section=5-why-does-fluid-leave-capillaries-even-when-protein-stays-in-blood\">→ Microcirculation, §5</a>)</em>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pc</strong>, capillary blood pressure: pushes out.</li>\n<li><strong>πc</strong>, plasma protein (albumin) pull: holds water in.</li>\n<li><strong>Pi</strong> and <strong>πi</strong>, the tissue's own pressure and protein pull: smaller.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Traditional Starling filtration model</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"J_v = K_f[(P_c − P_i) − σ(π_c − π_i)]\">J_v = K_f[(P_c − P_i) − σ(π_c − π_i)]</span></div>\n\n<p>Two extra terms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kf</strong>, how leaky the wall is (and how much wall there is). A leaky, inflamed wall has a high Kf.</li>\n<li><strong>σ</strong>, how well the wall holds back protein. <strong>σ = 1</strong> means proteins can't cross at all, so their full pull counts. In leaky, inflamed capillaries <strong>σ falls</strong>, proteins slip through, and their pull is lost.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"3-five-routes-to-the-same-outcome-excess-interstitial-fluid\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> Five routes to the same outcome: excess interstitial fluid</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>What fails</th>\n  <th>What changes</th>\n  <th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Push too high</strong></td>\n  <td>Pc ↑</td>\n  <td>Blocked veins, heart failure, standing still, leaky vein valves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Pull too weak</strong></td>\n  <td>πc ↓ (low albumin)</td>\n  <td>Liver disease, kidney protein loss (<span data-term=\"nephrotic\">nephrotic syndrome</span>), malnutrition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Wall too leaky</strong></td>\n  <td><span data-term=\"kf\">Kf</span> ↑, σ ↓</td>\n  <td>Inflammation, burns, allergy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Drain blocked</strong></td>\n  <td>Lymph flow ↓</td>\n  <td>Filariasis, lymph nodes removed in breast cancer surgery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Too much fluid overall</strong></td>\n  <td>Salt and water retained</td>\n  <td>Kidney failure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"starling-balance\" data-start=\"hf\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Starling balance: what tips it towards oedema?</figcaption><p>The Starling balance interactive from Microcirculation, opening on Heart failure. Each scenario button shows which arrow changes and the net result.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>The key question for any swelling: which part failed?</strong> Different diseases can look alike on the outside but work in completely different ways.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-why-does-raised-venous-pressure-cause-oedema\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> Why does raised venous pressure cause oedema?</h2>\n<p>If blood can't drain out of the veins, it <strong>backs up into the capillaries</strong>, raising Pc and pushing more fluid out. The lecture lists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blocked veins</strong> (a clot in a leg vein): one leg swells.</li>\n<li><strong>Leaky vein valves</strong> (varicose veins): blood falls back and pools.</li>\n<li><strong>Standing still for long periods</strong>: gravity raises leg venous pressure, and the muscle pump isn't working, so the ankles swell. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/venous-return?section=4-how-do-the-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-venous-valves-work\">→ Venous Return, §4</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Heart failure</strong>:<ul>\n<li>A failing <strong>right</strong> heart raises systemic venous pressure, causing swollen ankles.</li>\n<li>A failing <strong>left</strong> heart raises pressure in the lung veins, causing <strong>pulmonary oedema</strong>: fluid in the lungs, which stops oxygen getting into the blood and makes the patient breathless.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Arteriole and venule tone matter too.</strong> Widening the arterioles lets more of the arterial pressure through to the capillaries. Tightening the venules holds blood back in them. Both raise Pc, and the lecture lists both as causes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-can-low-plasma-proteins-cause-oedema\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Why can low plasma proteins cause oedema?</h2>\n<p><strong>Albumin is the main protein holding water in the blood.</strong> If it falls, that pull weakens and fluid stays out in the tissues, often all over the body.</p>\n<p>Albumin falls when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The liver can't make enough:</strong> liver failure (<span data-term=\"cirrhosis\">cirrhosis</span>).</li>\n<li><strong>The kidneys leak it into the urine:</strong> nephrotic syndrome.</li>\n<li><strong>The diet lacks protein:</strong> severe malnutrition (kwashiorkor).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture also mentions <strong>osmotically active substances building up in the tissues</strong>: they pull water out into the tissue from the other side.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-how-does-inflammation-increase-oedema\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> How does inflammation increase oedema?</h2>\n<p><strong>Histamine, <span data-term=\"bradykinin\">bradykinin</span> and <span data-term=\"substance-p\">substance P</span></strong> make the capillary wall <strong>leaky</strong>: the gaps between endothelial cells widen. Water and proteins pour out. The escaped proteins now pull water <strong>outwards</strong>, adding to the swelling.</p>\n<p>This is why a sting or a sprain swells within minutes. It is <strong>not a pressure problem; the wall itself has changed.</strong></p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"4\" data-part=\"Sections 1–6\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–6 · 4 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A man has a clot blocking the main vein of one leg. Why does that leg swell?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Blood backs up and raises capillary pressure, pushing more fluid out</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The clot releases proteins that pull water into the tissue</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The lymphatics in that leg are blocked by the clot</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Albumin in that leg is destroyed by the clot</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"edema:ea619eee\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> A blocked vein raises Pc upstream, so filtration rises in that leg only.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A child with severe protein malnutrition has a swollen belly and legs. What is the main mechanism?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Raised capillary blood pressure from heart failure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Low albumin weakens the pull holding water in the blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Inflammation makes every capillary leaky</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Blocked lymphatics in the abdomen</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"edema:f5e10d7f\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Without enough dietary protein, albumin falls, and the plasma oncotic pull weakens throughout the body.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why does a bee sting swell within minutes?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The sting raises blood pressure in the arm</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The venom blocks the local lymph vessels</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Histamine makes capillaries leaky, so fluid and proteins escape</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The sting lowers the level of plasma albumin</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"edema:12f3583f\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Inflammatory mediators open the gaps in the capillary wall. The leaked proteins then pull still more water out.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Why can left-sided heart failure make a patient breathless?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Fluid collects around the heart and squeezes it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Raised lung venous pressure pushes fluid into the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The right ventricle stops pumping blood to the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Low albumin causes swelling throughout the chest</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"edema:5696cadf\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Back pressure into the lung capillaries causes pulmonary oedema, which blocks oxygen getting into the blood.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"7-why-does-lymphatic-obstruction-cause-persistent-swelling\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> Why does lymphatic obstruction cause persistent swelling?</h2>\n<p>Even with normal pressures, some fluid and protein leave the capillaries all the time, and <strong>only the lymphatics can take the protein back</strong>. If they are blocked, protein-rich fluid builds up. Over time it causes inflammation and scarring, and the swelling becomes firm and permanent: <strong>lymphoedema</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lecture's examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Filariasis (<span data-term=\"elephantiasis\">elephantiasis</span>):</strong> parasitic worms block the lymph vessels, so the legs or scrotum swell enormously.</li>\n<li><strong>Radical mastectomy:</strong> removing the armpit lymph nodes in breast cancer surgery leaves that arm swollen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/lymphatics\">→ Lymphatics</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"8-why-are-pitting-and-non-pitting-oedema-different\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.</span> Why are pitting and non-pitting oedema different?</h2>\n<p>Press a thumb firmly into swollen skin for a few seconds.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pitting oedema:</strong> a dent stays after you let go. The extra fluid is free to move and is pushed aside. This is typical of heart failure, low albumin and venous causes.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-pitting oedema:</strong> no dent forms. The tissue is firm, because the fluid is bound up with protein and the tissue has become scarred.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs14-pitting-oedema\">\n<img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/chapter14/pitting-oedema.jpg\" alt=\"An examiner's thumbs press firmly into the tops of a child's swollen feet to test for pitting oedema.\" width=\"997\" height=\"727\">\n<figcaption><strong>Pitting oedema in both feet.</strong> Firm thumb pressure for a few seconds pushes the free interstitial fluid aside. When the thumb is lifted, a pit is left behind and fills in slowly as the fluid seeps back.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>Lymphoedema</strong> is usually non-pitting, but <strong>only once it is long-standing</strong>. Early <span data-term=\"lymphoedema\">lymphoedema</span> can still pit, and it becomes firm as scarring develops.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture states that lymphoedema does not pit. That describes its usual late stage; early lymphoedema can pit.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"9-which-location-of-oedema-suggests-which-mechanism\"><span class=\"sec-num\">9.</span> Which location of oedema suggests which mechanism?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Where the swelling is</th>\n  <th>What it suggests</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Both ankles, worse at the end of the day</strong></td>\n  <td>Gravity plus raised venous pressure (standing, right heart failure)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>The lungs</strong> (breathlessness, crackles)</td>\n  <td>Left heart failure, or leaky lung capillaries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>All over, including around the eyes</strong></td>\n  <td>Low albumin (kidney or liver disease, malnutrition)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>One arm or leg, firm</strong></td>\n  <td>Blocked lymphatics, or a blocked vein</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are clues, not diagnoses. Heart and kidney disease often cause oedema by <strong>more than one route at once</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 7–9\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 7–9 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 7–9</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A woman's arm has been firmly swollen since her armpit lymph nodes were removed. What is the mechanism?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Raised venous pressure from a clot in the arm</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Fluid and protein can't drain away through the lymphatics</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Low plasma albumin from the surgery</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Histamine released by the scar tissue</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"edema:4de48787\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> With the nodes removed, protein-rich fluid can't drain, so lymphoedema develops.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> You press on a patient's shin and a dent remains. What does that tell you?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The extra fluid is free to move and was pushed aside</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The tissue is scarred and firm</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The swelling must be caused by blocked lymphatics</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The patient's albumin level must be normal</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"edema:913d15b3\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Pitting means mobile fluid. It is typical of heart failure, low albumin and venous causes, and of early lymphoedema.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why do proteins matter so much in lymphatic oedema?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Only the lymphatics can return escaped proteins to the blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Proteins block the capillary pores and raise pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Lymph contains no protein, so it dilutes the tissue</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Proteins make the veins collapse and stop draining</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"edema:bce8f30e\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Capillaries can't take protein back. If the lymph is blocked, protein builds up and holds water in the tissue.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"10-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">10.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Oedema</strong> = too much fluid between the cells. More leaves the capillaries than the lymphatics can carry away.</li>\n<li><strong>Only two ways:</strong> too much filtration, or too little drainage.</li>\n<li><strong>Five routes:</strong> push too high (Pc ↑), pull too weak (albumin ↓), wall too leaky, drain blocked, or too much fluid overall.</li>\n<li><strong>Raised venous pressure:</strong> blocked veins, leaky valves, standing still, heart failure. Right failure → swollen ankles; left failure → pulmonary oedema.</li>\n<li><strong>Low albumin:</strong> liver failure, nephrotic syndrome, malnutrition → swelling everywhere.</li>\n<li><strong>Inflammation:</strong> histamine, bradykinin and substance P make the wall leaky; escaped proteins pull more water out.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphatic blockage:</strong> filariasis, removal of lymph nodes → protein-rich, firm lymphoedema.</li>\n<li><strong>Pitting</strong> = mobile fluid. <strong>Non-pitting</strong> = firm, scarred tissue (late lymphoedema).</li>\n<li><strong>Ask where the swelling is</strong>: it points to the mechanism that failed.</li>\n</ol>\n";
